  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.33.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. This pin skips v5.32.0, whose change is carried here too. v5.33.0 declares a line this distribution inherits without choosing it: the engine now amends a runtime's own tool text, where before its plugins only added behaviour of their own. The occasion was a real refusal. The runtime folds edit, write and apply_patch onto one edit permission when it decides which tools to send, so granting edit hands over apply_patch and no permission can withhold that one tool. Its description opens by telling the model to use it to edit files, and a model treated that as outranking its system prompt: it refused to edit a file on another host over SSH with the person's explicit authorization, and kept refusing after two releases said otherwise in the shared prompt. What binds it is the text arriving with the tool definitions, not the text arriving as a prompt, so the correction now travels on that same channel through the tool.definition hook. It appends a paragraph saying what the tool can reach; it never matches or rewrites the runtime's own sentence, because such a match would stop applying in silence the day the runtime rewords it. Proven by running it against the real case before it was shipped. The limit is part of the declaration and matters as much as the reason: the runtime's text is amended only when it contradicts what the product needs AND no permission lever exists. A permission that can be denied gets denied; a tool that can be withheld gets withheld. It is not a licence to reword whatever the runtime says. One consequence belongs to this distribution specifically. A rebrand substitutes .md and .txt only, so every .ts asset travels verbatim: the plugin's installed filename is derived from this identity, but nothing inside the file is. That was a comment in the engine until now and is a derived guard as of this release -- every bundled .ts and .js is scanned for the engine's brand, with two exemptions that each state why they are wire plumbing rather than a name anyone reads. Those two remain the accepted residue this distribution already reports. This distribution changed nothing of its own, as in 1.6.0 through 1.9.0: the work happened upstream and only the pin moved."
  }
}
